+# Native legacy passwords, local candidate
+
+Built against Stalwart v0.16.27, commit aa47d8e. This is a local candidate,
+not a production deployment. No real credential database was read or changed.
+
+## How it works
+
+Existing primary hashes are imported as ordinary native Password credentials.
+Existing additional hashes become ordinary native AppPassword credentials,
+with `{LEGACY-BCRYPT}` prepended to the raw bcrypt verifier in its existing
+secret field. There is no registry schema or binary serialization change.
+
+Login checks only marked AppPassword credentials on the resolved local account,
+hashing the complete client input. A match produces Stalwart's scoped token for
+that individual credential. Native expiry, allowed IPs, account permissions,
+alias policy and revocation continue to apply. Imported credentials cannot be
+used as master-user credentials. Existing native generated passwords still work.
+Native-looking generic primary/additional passwords are covered by regressions.
+Bcrypt candidate checks run on the blocking pool, not an async I/O worker.
+
+The one-off importer assigns inherited account permissions, as requested.
+It does not copy old unused protocol flags or grant any new account permissions.
+Names are retained with a source-ID/verifier-fingerprint provenance suffix for
+retry detection. After migration is final, descriptions can be renamed normally.
+
+## Temporary import mode
+
+Stalwart's registry normally rejects supplied additional-password secrets. Two
+small guards admit marked bcrypt hashes only while the server process has
+`STALWART_LEGACY_PASSWORD_IMPORT=1`. Account validation also requires the caller's
+native account-update permission, an internal directory and inherited permissions.
+API key import and unmarked/malformed additional secrets remain rejected.
+Credential IDs, creation timestamps, writes and cache invalidation are native.
+
+Remove that environment variable and restart after the approved import. Login
+does not require it. The gated import code can also be omitted from later builds;
+the marker recognition/login patch must remain while these passwords are used.
+
+## Disposable importer
+
+`legacy_password_import.py` uses Python's standard library. It opens the source
+compatibility SQLite database with `mode=ro`, and writes through native JMAP.
+It defaults to a dry run. It accepts only explicit loopback HTTP(S) endpoints,
+disables proxy environment use, refuses redirects and does not log secrets or
+raw error responses. The portable JSON input is useful for synthetic tests.
+
+Inputs:
+
+* A frozen SQLite compatibility database with the existing `objects` table, or
+  JSON with `mailbox`, `app`, `domain` arrays in the same field format.
+* A JSON mapping of source mailbox addresses to existing native Account IDs.
+* A mode-0600 JSON authentication file with `username` and `password` fields.
+
+Example against an independently authorized local test server:
+
+```sh
+python3 tools/legacy_password_import.py \
+  --source-db /absolute/private/path/credentials.sqlite \
+  --mapping /absolute/private/path/account-map.json \
+  --auth-file /absolute/private/path/admin.json \
+  --url http://127.0.0.1:8080/jmap/
+```
+
+`--apply` performs the import and reads back identities/counts/descriptions.
+Existing credentials are appended to, not replaced. Only the primary password
+secret is replaced with the source primary verifier. All mappings are validated
+before the first write. Each account update is atomic; the whole batch is not.
+Retry only against the same frozen snapshot. An uncertain HTTP response requires
+a dry run/reconciliation first. Freeze credential management during the import.
+Do not rerun after users begin managing or revoking their native credentials:
+the source is no longer authoritative after that point.
+
+Missing/inactive source mailboxes and domains, source primary MFA, duplicate
+source IDs and target identity/directory mismatches are refused. Revoked source
+additional passwords are omitted. Unknown/retired accounts are never selected
+implicitly. The importer does not modify roles, aliases, domains, quotas or mail.
+Its temporary Account import route does not apply AppPassword/set creation
+quotas. Existing imported passwords authenticate independently of that cap;
+future native creation still follows configured native quotas.
+
+## Reproduce local checks
+
+Use the locked dependencies. Rust 1.99.1 was used for this candidate. The local
+checks use only synthetic credentials and a disposable SQLite-backed server.
+Test listeners bind 127.0.0.1 on 8899, 18893, 18887 and 18890. Keep those ports
+free. The protocol checks authenticate and log out; no message is sent.
+
+```sh
+cargo build --locked -p stalwart --no-default-features --features sqlite,enterprise
+STALWART_LEGACY_PASSWORD_IMPORT=1 STORE=Sqlite \
+  cargo test --locked -p tests --no-default-features --features sqlite \
+  --test legacy_password -- --test-threads=1 --nocapture
+STORE=Sqlite cargo test --locked -p tests --no-default-features --features sqlite \
+  --test legacy_password -- --test-threads=1
+python3 -m unittest discover -s tools -p test_legacy_password_import.py -v
+```
+
+The enabled integration test invokes the actual Python importer against the
+patched server's real JMAP endpoint. It covers dry run, import, idempotent retry,
+unchanged $2y$ primary/additional password strings, native-format collisions,
+generated native passwords, cross-account/master-user refusal, unauthorized and
+invalid imports, expiry, IP restrictions, account disablement and individual
+revocation. Real IMAP, SMTP STARTTLS and Sieve TLS clients exercise the same
+credentials, including rejection after revocation. A single-thread runtime test
+also checks 12-candidate matching without blocking its timer.
+
+The disabled-mode run verifies import is closed by default, ordinary primary
+login still works, and existing marked hash verification needs no import flag.
+It does not simulate a production restart with a real credential database.
+
+Python tests cover input selection, revoked/inactive records, text MFA flags,
+strict hash formats, private files, endpoint restrictions and read-only SQLite.
+
+## Verification boundaries
+
+The local executable is a macOS ARM64 debug build with SQLite and enterprise
+features compiled in. Linux/RocksDB release builds, the production license,
+production hashes and actual user devices have not been exercised.
+
+The upstream broad `tests --lib` target currently fails to compile independently
+of this patch: `tests/src/webdav/cal_itip.rs:280` calls `itip_merge_changes` with
+two arguments, while the function requires three. The separate `legacy_password`
+test target reuses existing test helpers without changing that unrelated test.
+macOS also emits an oversized debug unwind-table linker warning; linking succeeds.
+
+Keep the small source patch rebased and tested for future Stalwart upgrades.
+Do not replace the running production binary or remove the existing gateway
+until a separate production rollout is explicitly authorized.
